# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# generated runtime asset namespaces (FOUNDATION-DEPLOYMENT-ISO-B3C1 / S3E1C)
#
# The deployment's `content/assets/**` is the ONE authority; the runtime namespaces are derived from it by
# `scripts/sync-runtime-assets.mjs`:
#
#   /public/assets/          the SHARED platform namespace (icon library, platform marks, and — in legacy
#                            mode — the replaceable role artwork)
#   /public/spokes/          one NAMESPACE PER DECLARED SPOKE (`/spokes/<runtime-segment>/assets`), so two
#                            Spokes may ship the same asset basename without either overwriting the other
#
# Both are regenerated by `pnpm install` (postinstall), `pnpm dev` and `pnpm build`, so a fresh clone never
# needs a committed copy — and nothing derived can disagree with its source in version control. Both rules
# are root-anchored: the deployment's own source tree (the capsule's
# `deployment/spokes/<spoke>/content/assets/**`) is NOT ignored by them.
/public/assets/
/public/spokes/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ── temporary project work — NEVER tracked (owner policy, GIT-TEMP-G1) ─────────────────────────────
# Never track temporary project work at any directory depth.
#
# A directory named `.temp` is where the OWNER hands material to and from a workspace: recovery sets,
# patches, inventories, scratch copies, handoffs. It is never source, authored content, configuration,
# build output or deployment state, and it must never enter version control — at the repository root or
# beneath ANY nested directory.
#
# `**/.temp/` and the shorter `.temp/` are equivalent in Git (a pattern with no separator other than
# the trailing one matches a directory of that name at ANY depth); the explicit `**/` form states the
# depth requirement rather than relying on that subtlety. An ANCHORED `/.temp/` is deliberately NOT
# used: it covers one directory level only, which is exactly how a nested `.temp` directory ends up
# untracked-but-stageable.
#
# An ignore rule cannot prevent a forced `git add -f`, so the exclusion is backed by
# `scripts/check-temp-tracking.mjs` — a read-only Git-INDEX guard run from CI's required `validate`
# path, which fails the run whenever any tracked path contains a `.temp` directory component.
**/.temp/
